---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DATA_SOURCES, SOURCE_CITATIONS, CITATION_NAME, UMBRELLA_NAME, DOI, DOI_URL, u } from "../lib/site";
import { REPORTS } from "../validation/registry";
import { TOOLS } from "../lib/tools";
import { NOTES } from "../lib/notes";
import { GSC_TOP20, NOTES_ROUND2 } from "../data/nuclide-notes";

/* ★ 보고서가 있는 도구 목록을 **글자로 들지 않는다** — 평가가 하나 늘 때마다 이 문장만
   낡고 게이트는 산문을 못 본다. 등록부에서 파생시키면 저절로 따라온다. */
const nameOf = (slug: string) => TOOLS.find((t) => t.slug === slug)?.name ?? slug;
const done = REPORTS.filter((r) => r.status === "published").map((r) => nameOf(r.tool));
/* ★ 도구 이름에 「and」가 들어 있어(「Decay and half-life」) 「A, B and C」로 이으면
   어디가 이름이고 어디가 접속사인지 안 갈린다 — 줄표로 감싸 쉼표로만 잇는다. */
const doneList = done.join(", ");
/* ★ 분모는 등록부가 아니라 **도구 목록**이다 — 등록부에 아직 없는 도구(X-ray 스펙트럼)가 빠지면
   「7 of the 7」처럼 전부 갖춘 것으로 읽힌다(2026-10-25). */
const publishedSlugs = new Set(REPORTS.filter((r) => r.status === "published").map((r) => r.tool));
const without = TOOLS.filter((t) => !publishedSlugs.has(t.slug)).map((t) => t.name);
---
<Base schema="TechArticle" title="Methods and data"
      description="Where the nuclear and attenuation data come from, how the gamma constants are computed, how the results are validated, and the known limitations.">
  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">Methods and data</h1>
  <section class="prose-doc mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>
      Nuclear decay data — half-lives, decay modes, photon energies and emission probabilities, beta
      maximum and mean energies with branch intensities, and alpha energies and intensities — are
      retrieved from the IAEA Nuclear Data Section's Livechart interface, which serves the evaluated
      nuclear structure data file (ENSDF). Photon mass attenuation coefficients and mass
      energy-absorption coefficients are taken from the NIST X-Ray Mass Attenuation Coefficients
      tables, covering 1 keV to 20 MeV, and are interpolated logarithmically in both energy and
      coefficient.
    </p>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      {DATA_SOURCES.map((d) => <li><a href={d.url} rel="noopener" class="underline">{d.name}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both datasets are harvested once and committed to the source repository rather than fetched at
      runtime. Results therefore do not change between visits, the site does not depend on an
      external service being up, and the data version is fixed and auditable.
    </p>

    <h2>Gamma constants</h2>
    <p>
      The air kerma rate constant is computed as Γ = Σ E<sub>i</sub>·y<sub>i</sub>·(µ<sub>en</sub>/ρ)<sub>air</sub>(E<sub>i</sub>) / 4π,
      summed over photon lines above the low-energy cutoff δ. The default δ is 20 keV, the
      conventional value used by published compilations. Because the sum is performed at calculation
      time rather than baked into the data, δ is adjustable in the shielding calculator.
    </p>

    <h2>Validation</h2>
    <p>
      The engine is covered by 99 automated tests that run on every change. Computed gamma constants
      are checked against published values for Cs-137, I-131, Cs-134, Co-60 and Ir-192 and agree
      within 2%. Specific activities for Co-60, Cs-137, Pu-239, U-238, Am-241, Sr-90 and H-3 agree
      within 1%. Beta maximum energies for fifteen nuclides and alpha principal energies for eight
      agree within 1% and 0.01% respectively. Attenuation coefficients are checked at five points
      against published values within 0.5%. Field rules of thumb are also checked for order of magnitude — one
      curie of Co-60 at one metre computes to 1.29 R/h against the remembered 1.32, and Cs-137 to
      0.326 against 0.33 — but a remembered number is not a citable source, and those two checks are
      labelled as what they are rather than as validation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Analytic identities are tested rather than asserted — one half-life leaves exactly half, one
      half-value layer transmits exactly 0.5, doubling the distance quarters the dose rate, and
      quadrupling the count time improves the detection limit by exactly two.
    </p>
    <p>
      Passing tests are evidence to whoever can read them. Where a calculation can be worked by hand
      from a published definition, the working itself is published, so that the result can be checked
      without taking the test suite on trust. The
      <a href={u("/validation/")} class="underline">validation reports</a> set out that working in
      full, name the sources each expected value comes from, and state how much of the tool has
      actually been covered. {done.length} of the {TOOLS.length} calculators have one —
      {doneList}.
      {without.length === 0
        ? "Every calculator is covered."
        : without.length === 1
          ? `The ${without[0]} has no worked report; its results rest on the automated tests and the check stated on its own page.`
          : `${without.join(", ")} have no worked report; their results rest on the automated tests and the checks stated on their own pages.`}
    </p>

    <h2>Known limitations</h2>
    <ul class="my-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>Point-source geometry in air. No room scatter, skyshine, source self-absorption or capsule.</li>
      <li>Attenuation without buildup is a narrow-beam result and underestimates behind thick shields.</li>
      <li>Buildup coefficients are not supplied; the Berger form accepts values you provide.</li>
      <li>Skin dose from surface contamination is not calculated.</li>
      <li>Decay chains assume a simple unbranched series.</li>
      <li>Beta range uses the Katz–Penfold empirical fit. It gives a depth of penetration, not the path length, and no accuracy is claimed for it here — see the validation report.</li>
      <li>Bremsstrahlung yield uses a thick-target approximation and is an order-of-magnitude guide.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to cite</h2>
    <p>
      If a figure from this site ends up in a report, a procedure or a paper, cite the source of the
      underlying data rather than only this site — the evaluated data are the IAEA's and NIST's work,
      and the value of a citation belongs with them. Cite this site in addition when it is the
      derivation, not the input, that you are relying on: the gamma constants, the spectrum-solved
      half-value layers and the per-line dose shares are computed here and appear in this form here.
    </p>

    <h3>This site</h3>
    <p class="my-3 rounded-md border border-line bg-surface px-3 py-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed">
      Min, J. <em>{CITATION_NAME} — nuclide reference sheets and spectrum-solved shielding for radiation
      protection</em>. {UMBRELLA_NAME}. <a href={DOI_URL} rel="noopener" class="underline">{DOI}</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The source and the data it carries are deposited with Zenodo, which mints the identifier above
      and keeps a copy independently of this site and of GitHub. Quote that identifier rather than a
      URL: it is the thing that is meant to still resolve when the address has moved.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a <em>concept</em> DOI, so it always resolves to the most recent deposit. If you need the
      exact state you worked from — because a number you quote must stay quotable — take the version
      DOI of that release from the record page instead. Both are listed there.
    </p>
    <p>
      The committed datasets do not change between visits. They are re-harvested when the upstream
      evaluations are updated, and each re-harvest is a new release with its own version DOI.
    </p>

    <h3>The decay data</h3>
    <p class="my-3 rounded-md border border-line bg-surface px-3 py-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed">
      {SOURCE_CITATIONS[0].text}
    </p>
    <p>{SOURCE_CITATIONS[0].terms}</p>

    <h3>The attenuation coefficients</h3>
    <p class="my-3 rounded-md border border-line bg-surface px-3 py-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed">
      {SOURCE_CITATIONS[1].text} <a href={SOURCE_CITATIONS[1].url} rel="noopener" class="underline">doi:10.18434/T4D01F</a>
    </p>
    <p>{SOURCE_CITATIONS[1].terms}</p>
    <p>
      Note that these tables are a NIST Standard Reference Database rather than a public-domain work
      of the United States government. A transcription of them is present in the public source tree,
      because the code computes with them; that file is covered by the NIST terms above and not by
      the MIT licence this site is released under, and it is not offered as a dataset or as standard
      reference data. Anyone wanting the tables themselves should take them from NIST.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not carried</h2>
    <p>
      {/* ★ 고아 쪽을 만들지 않는다 — 한계를 다루는 자리에서 잇는다. */}
      The formulas above describe what is computed. What the underlying table does not hold — decay
      chains past one step, branching ratios, photon lines below the cutoff, and the emissions no
      calculator here describes — is counted in
      {" "}<a href={u("/dataset-limits/")} class="underline">limits of this dataset</a>.
    </p>

    {/* ★ 2026-10-29(애드센스 재검토 준비 T10 F6 — 소유주 「추천대로」) — 무엇을 코드가 만들고 무엇을 AI 가 썼는지 밝힌다.
        출처를 단 해설(1차 T7 · 2차 T10-②)은 AI 가 출처로 쓰고 다른 AI 검토가 두 번 대조했다. 출처 없는 옛 해설은 그 대조를 거치지 않았다.
        사람이 쓰거나 검토했다고 적지 않는다(사실이 아니다). 핵종 쪽 출처 목록 아래 링크가 #writing 으로 온다.
        ★ 2026-10-29 소유주 통독 끝 — 출처를 단 40편(GSC_TOP20 + NOTES_ROUND2)을 처음부터 끝까지 읽었고 「데이터는 내가 가진 지식과 부합」.
        그래서 「저자가 읽었고 자기 지식과 맞았다」만 더한다(「썼다」 · 「검증했다」는 아니다). 수는 두 목록에서 센다 — 뒤에 늘어나는
        목록은 읽기 전까지 세지 않는다. 그 뒤에 더하거나 고친 해설은 AI 대조만 거쳤다고 밝힌다. */}
    <h2 id="writing">How the pages are written</h2>
    <p>
      The figures and tables on the calculator and nuclide pages, and the summary sentences at the top of
      each nuclide page, are computed from the data described above rather than written by hand, so a
      correction to the data carries through to every page that uses it. The prose around them, from the
      tool guides to the notes on each nuclide page, is written with the help of AI models. A note that
      ends with a list of sources was drafted from those sources; a separate AI review then checked it
      against them, and a second pass re-checked every sentence the first one changed. In October 2026 the
      author, who works in the radiation field, read all {GSC_TOP20.length + NOTES_ROUND2.length} notes that then carried a source list from
      start to finish and found the facts in them consistent with the author's own knowledge of the subject;
      a note added or changed since then has had only the AI checks until it is read again. Notes add
      context and change no computed value.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If a value here disagrees with a source you trust, the disagreement is worth reporting — several
      figures in the test suite exist because a check found an error during development. Contact
      details are on the <a href="/contact" class="underline">contact page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ★★ 읽을 글 — 2026-10-06 ⑥ 부터 이 쪽이 이 lab 안의 **허브**다. `/calc/` 허브가 걷혀 apex 홈으로 갔고(301),
      그 홈도 같은 목록을 들지만 lab 안에서 한 번에 닿는 자리가 하나는 있어야 한다(`check-notes` 가 잰다).
      정본은 `lib/notes.ts` — 글을 늘리면 거기에만 적는다. */}
  <section id="notes" class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-[18px] font-semibold text-ink">Longer notes</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      Questions the tools raise but cannot answer inside a form &mdash; where the reference sheets mislead,
      what to build a shield out of, which nuclides make good check sources, and where the data runs out.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {NOTES.map((n) => (
        <li class="card p-4 transition-colors hover:border-accent/50">
          <a href={u(n.path)} class="no-underline">
            <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{n.name}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-[13px] leading-snug text-ink-muted">{n.question}</p>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
